{"id": "c5f29344-385a-45dc-9870-98a7bf33185d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T01:51:27.745472", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c8a37e0e-4397-426b-8149-864fa3af7476", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-f0356e7c-bdc3-42e6-963c-637197aa7d13", "tool_call": {"id": "call-f0356e7c-bdc3-42e6-963c-637197aa7d13", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Propose TorchOpt-vs-autograd experiment approach\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"Inspection summary: /workspace contains only task.json (521 B); no TorchOpt source, READMEs, or track.md exist on disk. The only source material is the JSON, which fixes (i) \\\"reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation\\\" as the mechanism under test, (ii) the design-only deliverable, and (iii) CPU-only / 24-core / no-network runtime. I therefore propose the approach in API-category terms rather than fabricating TorchOpt symbols.\\n\\nProposed approach:\\n- Three branches: (A) TorchOpt explicit-diff/differentiable optimizer, (B) standard PyTorch autograd + optim.step, (C) same as B with create_graph=True as a control that isolates \\\"higher-order graph\\\" from \\\"intermediate-data reuse.\\\"\\n- CPU-only, pinned threads, two dtypes (float32 primary for stability, float64 for consistency reference).\\n- Three model regimes: small MLP, small CNN, ill-conditioned quadratic with swept condition number kappa.\\n- Sweeps: batch sizes {1,16,64,256,1024}, inner-loop length L in {1,5}, R=30 seeds.\\n- Four stability probes: seed-noise sweep, weight-perturbation sensitivity, gradient-norm trajectory conditioning, fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency.\\n- Identical seed/init/optimizer hyperparams; allocator-isolated subprocess per cell; zero_grad(set_to_none) toggled as a documented confound.\\n- Measurement: time.perf_counter_ns brackets around backward-only and backward+step, K=50 reps per cell, median + IQR; peak RSS via getrusage + tracemalloc; gc-filtered retained-tensor bytes as estimate.\\n- Stats: paired Wilcoxon on per-seed deltas, bootstrap 95% CI on median ratio, paired Cohen's d and Cliff's delta, Holm-Bonferroni over the model x batch x kappa x dtype family; pre-registered primary/secondary endpoints and decision rule.\\n\\nAssumptions (mine, separated from task-fixed constraints): A1 standard autograd = backward+step with create_graph=False; A2 TorchOpt API category = differentiable/functional optimizer wrapper; A3 allocator- and graph-bound benefits are device-agnostic; A4 stability probed primarily at float32 and high kappa; A5 paired-by-seed is the statistical unit; A6 memory only fully measurable via OS RSS + tracemalloc, retained-tensor count is an estimate.\\n\\nUncertainties needing your decision before I write the full design: U1 exact TorchOpt API symbol(s) to test; U2 whether the question targets single-step allocator reuse vs. meta-learning inner-outer gradient differentiation; U3 which stability metric(s) are primary; U4 compute budget for ~28.8k timing runs; U5 primary memory metric (RSS vs. tracemalloc); U6 deterministic single-thread vs. realistic 24-thread measurement arms.\",\"evidence\":\"Inspection commands and outputs: 'find /workspace -type f' returned only /workspace/task.json (521 B). 'ls -la /workspace' confirmed no README, no track.md, no TorchOpt source. 'cat /workspace/task.json' reproduced the 5-line JSON quoted above. Python 3.12.3 present; 'import torch' and 'import torchopt' both raised ModuleNotFoundError; nothing under /home, /tmp, /opt contains TorchOpt. The quoted JSON strings \\\"reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation\\\", \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\", \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\", and gpu_enabled/cpu_affinity_count/network=disabled are the only source-anchored constraints available; no TorchOpt symbols, API names, or 'standard autograd' definitions can be quoted from in-repo source so I deliberately left exact symbol names as U1 rather than inventing them.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"A6: OS-level peak RSS (getrusage) and Python tracemalloc peak are the only reliable memory metrics in this CPU-only setting; retained-tensor counts from gc are an estimate.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"A5: Per-seed paired analysis is the correct statistical unit; unpaired tests would conflate seed noise with branch differences.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A4: Numerical stability differences are most visible at float32 and high Hessian condition number; float64 acts as the consistency reference, not the primary stability probe.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A3: Allocator- and autograd-graph-level cost reductions observable on CPU mirror those on GPU for the purposes of this comparison.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A2: TorchOpt 'explicit gradient differentiation API' refers to the differentiable/functional-optimizer category (often 'Meta' in TorchOpt terminology); exact symbol name to be confirmed by researcher.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"A1: 'standard autograd' = loss.backward(create_graph=False) + optim.step(); create_graph=True is a control arm, not the comparator.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"U6: Should the primary measurement pin to a single thread (deterministic) or use all 24 cores (realistic)?</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"U5: Is OS-peak RSS or tracemalloc peak the primary memory metric when they disagree on allocator reuse?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U4: Is the full factorial ~28.8k timed runs acceptable, or should cells (kappa sweep, seed count R=30) be pruned?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U3: Which numerical-stability metric is primary - lower cross-seed loss variance, lower perturbation sensitivity, better convergence on ill-conditioned losses, or fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U2: Does the question emphasize single-step intermediate-data reuse, or meta-learning inner-outer differentiation through the optimizer?</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"U1: Which exact TorchOpt API symbol(s) does the 'explicit gradient differentiation API' refer to in this question?</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-71ca7156-f1bb-4da4-ae9b-9c1267001876", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
