{"id": "c969bb31-413d-4a28-9def-45eae902174a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T01:52:43.515411", "source": "user", "parent_id": "361108a8-f26e-42d1-b4c3-c223e22b3a5d", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with branch structure A/B/C (TorchOpt differentiable-optimizer family / standard autograd + optim.step / standard autograd + create_graph=True isolation control). Proceed to produce the full experiment-design deliverable as a single document, structured roughly as: (1) Objective and pre-registered primary/secondary endpoints, (2) Comparator definitions and API contracts, (3) Independent variables and sweep grid, (4) Stability probes, (5) Performance & memory measurement protocol, (6) Statistical analysis plan, (7) Controls and confounds, (8) Feasibility and runtime budget estimate, (9) Threats to validity, (10) Pre-registration of primary endpoint and decision rule. Concrete guidance to fold in: (U1) Since no TorchOpt source is on disk and execution is not required, describe the TorchOpt comparator using an API-category interface contract (e.g., 'functional/differentiable optimizer that exposes a differentiable routine taking current params and a per-sample functional loss, returning updated params and returning intermediates through the same compute graph as the forward pass') rather than fabricated symbol names; list candidate symbol families (Meta, RunMeta, differentiable) as illustrative, marked explicitly as deferred to a real implementation. (U3) Pre-register perturbation sensitivity (norm of gradient change under fixed relative weight perturbation, averaged over seeds) as the primary numerical-stability endpoint; treat seed-noise loss variance and fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency as co-secondary. (U4) Size the design against the 24-core CPU, no-network, design-only constraint: report the full factorial cell count, a minimally-feasible core cell set (e.g., one model regime × key batch sizes × at least 3 κ values), and a rationale for any pruning, so the document remains a credible protocol without claiming execution. (U6) Make single-thread-bound timing the primary wall-clock protocol (deterministic, reproducible) and report 24-core wall-clock as an informational secondary; pre-declare this triage so reviewers can interpret ratios. End the document with a short 'Open items' section listing U1, U2 and anything else deferred to a real implementation, and explicitly restate that no experiment was executed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 28}
{"id": "3cb369af-f999-44fe-9ab8-c8ce11edd531", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T01:54:12.587539", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c969bb31-413d-4a28-9def-45eae902174a", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: TorchOpt Explicit-Differentiation API vs. Standard PyTorch Autograd\n\n**Status:** Design only. No experiment was executed. No results are reported. All numeric projections in this document are protocol specifications, not measurements.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Objective and Pre-Registered Endpoints\n\n### 1.1 Research question (as posed)\n\nDoes the explicit gradient differentiation API in TorchOpt — which the source claim says \"reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation\" — reduce computation time and improve numerical stability versus standard PyTorch autograd?\n\n### 1.2 Pre-registered endpoints\n\n**Primary performance endpoint (P-Perf)**\nPer-iteration wall-clock time of the (backward + optimizer step) region, measured single-thread pinned, reported as the median across K=50 timed iterations per seed, then aggregated as the median across R=30 seeds. Reported both as raw nanoseconds and as the paired ratio `(Branch A) / (Branch B)`.\n\n**Primary numerical-stability endpoint (P-Stab) — per U3**\nMean gradient-norm response to a fixed relative weight perturbation, averaged across seeds and across M=8 random perturbation directions per seed. Concretely: for each seed s and each direction e generated from a separated RNG stream,\n    sens(s, e) = ‖∇L(θ + θ⊙e) − ∇L(θ)‖ / ‖∇L(θ)‖\nand the cell-level endpoint is the mean of sens(s, e) over all (s, e) pairs. Lower is more stable. Reported as paired difference `Branch A − Branch B`.\n\n**Co-secondary endpoints (S1, S2, S3)**\n- **S1 — Cross-seed loss-curve variance.** Standard deviation across the R=30 seeds of the integrated loss-trajectory AUC(∇¬L) at matched cell, paired.\n- **S2 — fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency.** ‖∇L_fp32 − cast(∇L_fp64)‖ / ‖∇L_fp64‖, evaluated on identical inputs and identical parameter tensors; serves as a consistency reference for S2 ≤ 10⁻⁵ in fp64-imitating branches (sanity) and as a stability probe in fp32.\n- **S3 — Backward-only wall-clock and peak memory.** Reported as auxiliary per-phase attribution so that any aggregate time win can be attributed to the backward phase, the optimizer-step phase, or both.\n\n**Informational secondary endpoint (I-Perf — per U6)**\nWall-clock time under full 24-thread fan-out, unpinned, single-shot per cell. Reported only as an informational indicator; not part of the primary decision rule.\n\n### 1.3 Direction of effect\n\nP-Perf win: `(A)/(B) < 1` is desired. P-Stab win: `(A − B) < 0` is desired. Both directed; pre-registered.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comparator Definitions and API Contracts\n\n### 2.1 Branch A — TorchOpt explicit-differentiation API\n\n**API-category interface contract (used as the source of truth in place of any fabricated symbol):**\n\nThe comparator is a \"functional / differentiable optimizer\" of the form:\n\n    meta_params = wrap(params)               # params re-bound as meta-params\n    fast_params = tracer.trace_state(meta_params)\n    for inner_step in range(L):\n        loss_inner = functional_loss(fast_params, x_inner)\n        fast_params = functional_step(fast_params, loss_inner)   # in-graph step\n    loss_outer = outer_loss(model(fast_params), x_outer)\n    loss_outer.backward()                    # gradients flow back through in-graph step\n    outer_opt.step(meta_params)\n\nTwo properties are required by the contract and must be enforced by the eventual implementation:\n\n1. **In-graph reuse.** `functional_step` returns `fast_params` whose `.grad_fn` is a node in the autograd graph, and the saved tensors inside that node are *the same buffers used by the next iteration's forward*, not fresh clones.\n2. **No re-materialization on the inner path.** The forward pass over the inner dataset is not re-evaluated when computing meta-gradients; the inverse-VJP is computed against the saved intermediates only. This is the operational meaning of \"reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation\" as it appears in the prompt.\n\n**Candidate symbol families (illustrative, NOT pre-registered as the exact API):** the `Meta*` family (e.g., the documented pattern of `MetaSGD` / `MetaAdam` / `MetaOptimizer`), the `RunMeta` family covering meta-gradient inner loops, and the lower-level `differentiable` functional-optimizer helpers. The exact symbol is **deferred to the real implementation** (see Open Items U1). What is fixed is the *interface contract above*, not the symbol.\n\n### 2.2 Branch B — Standard PyTorch autograd + optim.step\n\n    y = model(x)\n    loss = criterion(y, t)\n    loss.backward()\n    opt.step()\n    opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=<toggleable>)\n\n`create_graph=False`. This is the pre-registered definition of \"standard autograd\" (see Open Items U2 for the meta-learning read of the question).\n\n### 2.3 Branch C — Isolation control\n\nIdentical to Branch B except `loss.backward(create_graph=True)`. Purpose: separate the static cost of higher-order-graph construction from any explicit-diff reuse benefit. Branch C is the only one that builds a higher-order graph in either the standard side or the TorchOpt side; this arm disentangles \"allocations from graph build\" from \"allocations from inner-loop materialization\" so that the Branch-A win, if any, can be attributed correctly.\n\n### 2.4 API contracts that the implementation MUST honor\n\n- Identity-preserving optimizer state. A and B share the same base optimizer hyperparameters (lr, momentum, betas, eps, weight_decay) when one exists. Otherwise both are SGD with the same lr.\n- Identity-preserving forward graph. No `torch.no_grad()`, no `.detach()` in either branch's hot loop, except as required by each branch's documented usage and explicitly enumerated in the confounds log.\n- Identity-preserving init. Branch A and Branch B start from a shared state-dict that is held on the seed stream.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Independent Variables and Sweep Grid\n\n### 3.1 Variables\n\n| Variable | Levels | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Branch | A, B, C | Comparators |\n| Model regime | MLP, small CNN, ill-conditioned quadratic | Three regimes: clean flow, convolution graph shape, deliberately bad conditioning |\n| Batch size | {1, 16, 64, 256, 1024} | Per-sample → large-batch scaling of allocator and reuse benefit |\n| Inner-loop length L | {1, 5} | Tests \"single-step reuse\" vs. \"L-step unrolled reuse\"; L=5 is the meta-learning read of the question |\n| Hessian condition κ | {10², 10⁴, 10⁶, 10⁸} | Applied only in the ill-conditioned-quadratic regime; tunes stability stress |\n| Dtype | fp32 (timing), fp64 (consistency reference only) | Per U3 |\n| Seeds | R = 30 | Per-seed paired power target: d ≥ 0.5 at α=0.05, power ≥ 0.8 (Wilcoxon) |\n| Perturbation directions M | 8 per seed | For P-Stab, separable RNG stream |\n\n### 3.2 Full factorial cell count\n\nA \"cell\" is one (branch, model, batch, L, dtype, [κ]) combination. The κ dimension applies only to the quadratic model.\n\n- MLP: 5 batches × 2 L × 1 dtype × 30 seeds = 300 (timing) cells per branch\n- CNN: 5 batches × 2 L × 1 dtype × 30 seeds = 300 per branch\n- Quadratic: 5 batches × 2 L × 1 dtype × 4 κ × 30 seeds = 1200 per branch\n- Per branch: 1800 cells\n- Three branches: 5400 cells\n\nEach cell runs once on the single-thread pinned protocol with W=5 warm-up iterations and K=50 timed iterations. The fp64 dtype is only used for the S2 consistency probe and is not included in timing cells. Timing-iteration count for the protocol:\n\n    total_iters = 5400 cells × 30 seeds × (5 warm-up + 50 timed) iterations = 8,910,000 forward/backward/step calls\n\nIncluding L=5, Branch A and Branch C burn 5× the inner work per outer step.\n\n### 3.3 Minimally-feasible core (per U4)\n\nConsidering the CPU-only, 24-core, no-network, design-only constraint, the document defines a Core protocol that can be defended as a credible answer without running the full factorial:\n\n- One model regime — the ill-conditioned quadratic (the most stability-sensitive regime and therefore the most diagnostic).\n- Three κ values — {10², 10⁶, 10⁸}. These cover \"easy\", \"challenging\", and \"very ill-conditioned\".\n- Three batch sizes — {1, 64, 1024}. These cover per-sample, mid, and large.\n- Two L values — {1, 5}. These separate the single-step reuse claim from the L-unrolled reuse claim.\n- One branch set — {A, B} only at Core level. Branch C is reported only at the Quadratic/κ=10⁶/(batch=64, L=1) cell to confirm the higher-order-graph isolation logic.\n- Seeds R = 30.\n- Dtype fp32.\n\nCore timing cells per branch: 3 κ × 3 batches × 2 L × 30 seeds = 540. Pair (A vs B): 540 × 2 = 1080 timed cells, plus the single 1-cell Branch C sanity check. Core iterations: 1080 × (5 + 50) = 59,400 calls. Including L=5 amplification on Branch A and Branch C: total Core work ≈ 59,400 × (1 + ¼·4) ≈ 119k effective forward/backward calls. This is the size of the document's *committed* protocol; the full factorial remains the design's upper bound.\n\n**Pruning rationale.** The pruning keeps all models in the upper-bound specification but commits the Core to the regime most diagnostic of numerical stability (ill-conditioned quadratic), and keeps Batch × L coverage broad enough to demonstrate the L amplification effect. Models MLP and CNN are *retained* in the full factorial but moved to the \"informational\" tier so that the published analysis does not depend on them for the primary endpoint.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Stability Probes\n\n### 4.1 P-Stab: relative-weight perturbation sensitivity (PRIMARY, per U3)\n\nFor each seed s:\n1. Train to a fixed checkpoint t* (pre-registered t* = 50 outer steps).\n2. Clone θ to θ′ and θ″ via separate RNG.\n3. Direction e ~ UnitNormal(seed_s, m) with m = 1..M.\n4. θ_p = θ + θ ⊙ (ε · e), where ε = 1e-2 by default.\n5. Compute sens(s, e) = ‖∇L(θ_p) − ∇L(θ)‖ / ‖∇L(θ)‖.\n6. Cell-level value = mean over M.\n7. Branch-level value = mean over s.\n\nReported for both ε=1e-2 and ε=1e-4 (a coarse sweep). Lower is better.\n\n### 4.2 S1: cross-seed loss-curve variance\n\nEach cell computes R=30 trajectories of `(L_t, ‖∇L_t‖)` over T=200 outer steps. Reported endpoints: σ(AUC(L_t)) across seeds, paired; σ(AUC(‖∇L_t‖)) across seeds, paired.\n\n### 4.3 S2: fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency\n\nRun a single forward/backward in fp32 and in fp64, on identical inputs. The fp64 gradient is the reference. Report `‖∇L_fp32 − ∇L_fp64‖ / ‖∇L_fp64‖`. Run once per cell. In Branch C, fp64 expected to be ~1e-7; fp32 expected to be ~1e-3 to 1e-7 depending on loss conditioning.\n\n### 4.4 Auxiliary stability probes\n\n- Gradient-norm trajectory smoothness: per-step Δlog‖∇L‖ distribution.\n- Hessian condition proxy (Hutchinson) at t*; reported as cell metadata, not an endpoint.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Performance and Memory Measurement Protocol\n\n### 5.1 Wall-clock (per U6 — single-thread bound as PRIMARY)\n\n- Set `torch.set_num_threads(1)`, plus the equivalent NumPy/BLAS threading knobs via env vars (`OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1`).\n- Pin to a single core via `taskset -c <core>` in the subprocess.\n- Bracket two regions per iteration with `time.perf_counter_ns()`:\n  - **Region R1.** `loss = criterion(model(x), t); loss.backward()` — backward only.\n  - **Region R2.** `loss.backward() ; opt.step() ; opt.zero_grad(...)` — backward + step.\n- W=5 un-timed warm-up iterations, then K=50 timed iterations.\n- Per-seed summary: median and IQR of K values, separately for R1 and R2.\n- Per-cell summary: median across R=30 seeds of the per-seed medians.\n\n**Informational 24-core wall-clock (I-Perf).** Same protocol except `torch.set_num_threads` returns to the system default and no pinning is applied. Reported as median ns/iter ± IQR. Not part of the primary decision rule.\n\n### 5.2 Synchronization on CPU\n\nThere is no `cuda.synchronize` on CPU. The protocol uses `torch.set_num_threads(1)` + a tight barrier at the start of each timed iteration. Variance from the OS scheduler is absorbed by the K=50 repetitions and the median aggregate.\n\n### 5.3 Memory\n\nThree complementary measures per cell:\n\n1. **OS peak RSS.** `resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss` per subprocess. Subprocess isolation prevents allocator-cache carryover between cells, so RSS differences reflect the algorithm, not the cache.\n2. **Python tracemalloc peak.** Start `tracemalloc.start()` after warm-up; snapshot at the end of the K=50 timed iterations; report peak and current.\n3. **Retained-tensor bytes (estimate).** At the same checkpoint, snapshot `gc.get_objects()` filtered to `torch.Tensor` instances and sum `t.element_size() * t.nelement()` over each branch's live tensor set. Marked as estimate because `gc` walks are non-deterministic.\n\nA fourth, **allocator-namespace-specific** probe is included only where the implementation exposes a meaningful count (e.g., instrumented torchopt or a wrapped `cudaCachingAllocator` analogue for CPU). It is documented as a feature the protocol will use if available and as a no-op otherwise.\n\n### 5.4 Sequence isolation\n\nEach (cell, seed) pair is run in a fresh subprocess so that allocator state, autograd graph caches, and tracemalloc bookkeeping cannot leak across cells. Cell-level orchestration is the only place where numbers from different seeds are aggregated.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Statistical Analysis Plan\n\n### 6.1 Unit of analysis\n\nPer-seed paired. Each seed trains in parallel on A, B, and (when applicable) C, with identical init. Every aggregate statistic is computed on R=30 paired differences.\n\n### 6.2 Tests\n\n- **P-Perf.** Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-seed medians (paired). Bootstrap 95% CI on the median ratio `r_s = median_k(A)_s / median_k(B)_s`, B=10,000 resamples. Report paired Cohen's d_z and Cliff's δ for effect size.\n- **P-Stab.** Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-cell sens values. Bootstrap CI on the median paired difference. Effect sizes as above. Direction-preregistered: A − B < 0 is a stability win.\n- **S1, S2.** Wilcoxon signed-rank as for P-Stab; direction is \"smaller is better\".\n- **S3 / I-Perf.** Reported but not hypothesis-tested in the primary analysis.\n\n### 6.3 Multiple-comparison correction\n\nThe published family is `(model × batch × κ × L × {P-Perf, P-Stab, S1, S2})`. Holm–Bonferroni correction is applied over this family. Family-wise α = 0.05.\n\n### 6.4 Pre-declared subgroup analyses\n\n- By batch size (interaction test: does the A-vs-B gap scale with batch?).\n- By L (does the gap narrow or widen as the inner loop unrolls?).\n- By κ in the quadratic regime (does the stability benefit increase with conditioning?).\n\n### 6.5 Sensitivity analyses\n\n- Drop the worst seed (highest Huber residual of medians across branches) and recompute the headlined effect.\n- Drop the worst 10% of K=50 timed iterations within each seed and recompute.\n- Reanalyze with `zero_grad(set_to_none=False)` data only and then with `set_to_none=True` only, to test the allocator-cache confound.\n\n### 6.6 Pre-registered decision rule (restated in §10)\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls and Confounds\n\n| Confound | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Different default dtype handling | Both branches run fp32 by default; fp64 reserved for S2 only. |\n| Optimizer state mismatch | Same base optimizer in A and B; same hyperparameters. |\n| Init mismatch | Shared state-dict per seed, cloned via `state_dict()` swap before training starts. |\n| `zero_grad(set_to_none)` cache side-effect | Toggled; both settings reported; sensitivity analysis (§6.5). |\n| Autograd graph cache state across cells | Subprocess isolation; warm-up 5 iterations per cell. |\n| Forward-pass purity | No `no_grad`, no `detach` in the hot loop, except where the comparator contract requires it; enumerated. |\n| Higher-order graph cost | Branch C isolation, required. |\n| Scheduler jitter | Median over K=50 repetitions; single-thread pin in primary protocol. |\n| MKL/BLAS thread dispersion | `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS` all set to 1 in primary. |\n| Stability probe injection | Perturbation directions drawn from a perturbation-only RNG stream separated from the training RNG. |\n| Hidden TorchOpt internal allocations | Documented in Open Items U1; the protocol can only report what is observable. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility and Runtime Budget\n\n**Environment.** CPU-only, 24 cores, no network. Pure-Python orchestration; no GPU; no dataset downloads.\n\n**Per-cell work in primary protocol (single thread).**\n- One (cell, seed) pair runs K=55 iterations (5 warm-up + 50 timed) of forward + backward + step.\n- With L=5 on Branches A and C, an additional 4 inner steps per outer step are inserted.\n- Estimated per-iteration cost on the Core protocol at single-thread: ~5–25 ms for the quadratic regime, ~10–50 ms for MLP, ~20–80 ms for CNN.\n\n**Core protocol cost (estimated at design stage; not measured).**\n- 540 cells per branch × 2 branches = 1080 cells × 30 seeds = 32,400 (cell, seed) runs.\n- Per run: average ~3 s wall-clock (W+K = 55 iterations at ~50 ms each).\n- Total Core wall-clock at single-thread pin: 32,400 × 3 s = ~27 hours single-thread. With 24 cores, perfect parallelism would bring this to ~1.1 hours; realistic utilization of ~70% puts it at ~1.6 hours. The Core protocol fits within a single CPU-day.\n\n**Full factorial cost.** 5400 cells × 30 seeds × ~3 s ÷ 24 cores at 70% utilization ≈ ~8 hours CPU-time per-branch, ~24 hours total. Larger than Core but still ≤1 CPU-day wall-clock at 24-core fan-out.\n\n**Cost of the fp64 consistency probe.** 5400 cells × 1 fp64 call each. Negligible relative to the timing cells.\n\n**Cost of the stability probes.** P-Stab adds M=8 perturbation evaluations per cell, R1+R2-only; this is R1-only × M=8 × 5400 cells = 43,200 single-region calls. Negligible relative to timing work.\n\n**Decision.** Core protocol is the published commitment. Full factorial is the upper bound retained for documentation. Branch C is restricted to a single-cell sanity confirmation in Core.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Threats to Validity\n\n1. **Device generalization.** The single-thread pinned protocol is the reproducibility anchor but does not reflect realistic 24-core or GPU behavior. Mitigation: I-Perf reports an informational 24-core number; per U6, that number is not the primary endpoint.\n2. **TorchOpt version sensitivity.** No TorchOpt source is in scope, so the implementation of \"reuse intermediates\" may differ across TorchOpt point releases and the protocol may be measuring release-specific behavior. Mitigation: log the exact TorchOpt version, PyTorch version, and Python version in every run summary.\n3. **Allocator library influence.** PyTorch's CPU allocator and OS malloc behavior affects tracemalloc and retained-tensor estimates. Mitigation: subprocess isolation and three complementary memory measures (§5.3), none of which an OS-level decision can invalidate independently.\n4. **The \"reuse intermediate data\" benefit reinterpreted as allocator-cache warm-up.** A branch that reuses buffers may appear faster simply because it warms the malloc cache once and never re-touches free-list overhead. Mitigation: W=5 warm-up iterations plus the seed-replicated paired design; if the benefit survives warm-up, it cannot be solely attributable to first-call cache effects.\n5. **The functional-optimizer contract may not perfectly match real symbol semantics.** No source quotation is possible (U1). Mitigation: the contract in §2.1 is the protocol's source of truth, not any specific symbol. A real execution must map the contract to the chosen symbol and disclose any deviation in the confounds log.\n6. **Numerical stability under fp32 may be dominated by PyTorch's per-op kernels, not by the autograd graph.** Mitigation: include the ill-conditioned-quadratic regime at high κ, where the loss gradient amplifies any accumulated numerical error.\n7. **Confounding between L=5 inner-loop unrolling and explicit-diff reuse benefit.** Mitigation: branch C in its single-cell sanity role, plus the L=1 vs L=5 subgroup analysis.\n8. **OS-level background process interference.** Unmitigable but absorbed by the K-repetition median and the independent subprocess per cell.\n9. **Single-CPU bias for BLAS.** The single-thread pin is required for reproducibility but underutilizes the 24-core budget for the timing kernel. Mitigation: I-Perf as the parallel arm.\n10. **Implementation errors in the perturbation sensitivity probe.** Mitigation: print-assert that ‖∇L(θ)‖ is finite before dividing; clamp the per-cell value if it is not.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Pre-Registration of Primary Endpoint and Decision Rule\n\n### 10.1 Primary endpoint (singular, jointly pre-registered)\n\n- **P-Perf.** Per-iteration wall-clock of (backward + step) under single-thread pin, summarized as median-of-medians across R=30 seeds, reported as the ratio `r = median_A / median_B`. Direction-pre-registered: r < 1.\n- **P-Stab.** Mean relative-weight perturbation sensitivity (ε=1e-2, M=8), per seed, summarized as median-of-means across R=30 seeds, reported as paired difference `d = mean_A − mean_B`. Direction-pre-registered: d < 0.\n\n### 10.2 Decision rule\n\nFor each cell in the Core protocol, the protocol declares:\n\n- **TIME WIN** if and only if: `r_point < 1` AND `r_CI_high < 1` (two-sided 95% bootstrap CI on the median of `r_s` across seeds lies entirely below 1) AND Holm-corrected `p_Wilcoxon_Perf < 0.05`.\n- **STABILITY WIN** if and only if: `d_point < 0` AND `d_CI_high < 0` (two-sided 95% bootstrap CI on the median of `d_s` lies entirely below 0) AND Holm-corrected `p_Wilcoxon_Stab < 0.05`.\n- **BOTH WIN** claims the joint advantage.\n- **INCONCLUSIVE** in all other cases — including scenarios where direction matches but the CI crosses zero, or Holm correction nullifies an uncorrected finding.\n\n### 10.3 Pre-registration scope\n\nThe decision rule is locked at design time. Any deviation (e.g., adding a model regime, switching κ values, modifying ε) requires a new pre-registration record and is treated as a sensitivity analysis, not a primary test.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Open Items\n\n- **U1 — TorchOpt API symbol resolution.** The comparator is described by interface contract, not by symbol. Candidate families (`Meta*`, `RunMeta`, `differentiable`) are illustrative only. A real implementation must select a symbol, document any contract deviation, and re-run the confounds log.\n- **U2 — Reading of the question.** Two readings are possible: (a) single backward+step with allocator-level reuse benefit, and (b) meta-learning inner-outer loop with in-graph differentiation through the optimizer. The protocol covers both by including L ∈ {1, 5} and the Branch C isolation arm. Confirmation of which reading the question intends is still open.\n- **U3 — Stability primary endpoint.** Per the researcher's instruction, the primary stability endpoint is perturbation sensitivity (S0 in this document); cross-seed variance (S1) and fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency (S2) are co-secondary. This choice is locked.\n- **U4 — Factorial size.** Per the researcher's instruction, the document preserves the full factorial as the upper bound and commits the published Core protocol to Quadratic × 3 batches × 3 κ × 2 L. Pruning rationale is documented in §3.3 and §8. Any further pruning before publication is a pre-registration amendment.\n- **U5 — Memory primary metric.** The protocol reports three complementary memory numbers (RSS, tracemalloc peak, retained-tensor bytes). OS-peak RSS is the most reproducible across runs; tracemalloc peak is the most sensitive to allocator-level reuse; retained-tensor bytes is the most direct but is an estimate. The confusion matrix for these is documented but no single dominant metric is locked.\n- **U6 — Threading.** Per the researcher's instruction, single-thread pinned is the PRIMARY wall-clock protocol; 24-core wall-clock is INFORMATIONAL and secondary. This triage is pre-declared in §1.2, §5, §10.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Restatement of Design-Only Constraint\n\nThis document specifies an experiment. **No experiment was executed in producing this document.** All cell counts, timings, and effect sizes mentioned above are protocol parameters or design-stage projections, not measurements. The task context explicitly designates this as a design-only deliverable, and this document complies with that constraint throughout.", "evidence": "Inspection evidence from the planning phase remains controlling: /workspace contains only /workspace/task.json (521 B). No TorchOpt source, no README, no track.md is on disk. Python 3.12.3 is present but neither torch nor torchopt is importable, and the network is disabled, so even an exploratory import was impossible. The task.json quotes 'reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation' as the mechanism, declares the deliverable design-only ('Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'), and pins the runtime to CPU-only with 24 cores. Because no TorchOpt symbols exist on disk to quote, this design uses an interface contract (functional/differentiable optimizer taking meta-params and producing in-graph updated params) as the source of truth and lists Meta / RunMeta / differentiable families only as illustrative candidates, in line with U1. The Core protocol's pruning rationale (single model regime, three kappas, three batches, two L values, R=30 seeds, single branch set for primary test plus one Branch C sanity cell) is constructed to fit within the 24-core CPU no-network constraint and is documented in sections 3.3 and 8.", "assumptions": ["A1: standard autograd means loss.backward(create_graph=False) plus optim.step(); create_graph=True defines Branch C as an isolation control, not the comparator.", "A2: TorchOpt explicit gradient differentiation API refers to the differentiable / functional-optimizer category; the exact symbol is described by an interface contract rather than a fabricated name and is deferred to a real implementation.", "A3: Allocator- and autograd-graph-level cost reductions measurable on CPU mirror those on GPU in a way that supports a CPU-only design as a credible comparator.", "A4: Numerical stability differences are most visible at float32 and at high Hessian condition number; float64 is the consistency reference for S2, not the primary stability probe.", "A5: Per-seed paired analysis is the correct statistical unit; unpaired tests would conflate seed noise with branch differences.", "A6: Single-thread pinned wall-clock is the reproducibility anchor for the primary performance endpoint and is pre-declared as such; 24-core results are informational.", "A7: Subprocess isolation is required per (cell, seed) pair to prevent allocator and autograd-graph cache carryover across measurements.", "A8: Memory is most credibly reported via three complementary measures (OS RSS, tracemalloc peak, retained-tensor bytes) because PyTorch CPU does not expose a clean per-op allocator stat."], "uncertainties": ["U1: Which exact TorchOpt API symbol implements the interface contract in Section 2.1; deferred to a real implementation that maps the contract onto a symbol and documents any deviation.", "U2: Whether the question emphasizes single-step intermediate-data reuse or meta-learning inner-outer differentiation; the L in {1,5} sweep plus Branch C isolation covers both but does not resolve the ambiguity.", "U5: Whether OS-peak RSS, tracemalloc peak, or retained-tensor bytes is the primary memory metric when the three disagree on allocator reuse; the protocol currently reports all three."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-236e12e3-2472-467e-94cf-b9abca7c20d4", "tool_call": {"id": "call-236e12e3-2472-467e-94cf-b9abca7c20d4", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full TorchOpt-vs-autograd experiment design\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design: TorchOpt Explicit-Differentiation API vs. Standard PyTorch Autograd\\n\\n**Status:** Design only. No experiment was executed. No results are reported. All numeric projections in this document are protocol specifications, not measurements.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Objective and Pre-Registered Endpoints\\n\\n### 1.1 Research question (as posed)\\n\\nDoes the explicit gradient differentiation API in TorchOpt \\u2014 which the source claim says \\\"reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation\\\" \\u2014 reduce computation time and improve numerical stability versus standard PyTorch autograd?\\n\\n### 1.2 Pre-registered endpoints\\n\\n**Primary performance endpoint (P-Perf)**\\nPer-iteration wall-clock time of the (backward + optimizer step) region, measured single-thread pinned, reported as the median across K=50 timed iterations per seed, then aggregated as the median across R=30 seeds. Reported both as raw nanoseconds and as the paired ratio `(Branch A) / (Branch B)`.\\n\\n**Primary numerical-stability endpoint (P-Stab) \\u2014 per U3**\\nMean gradient-norm response to a fixed relative weight perturbation, averaged across seeds and across M=8 random perturbation directions per seed. Concretely: for each seed s and each direction e generated from a separated RNG stream,\\n    sens(s, e) = \\u2016\\u2207L(\\u03b8 + \\u03b8\\u2299e) \\u2212 \\u2207L(\\u03b8)\\u2016 / \\u2016\\u2207L(\\u03b8)\\u2016\\nand the cell-level endpoint is the mean of sens(s, e) over all (s, e) pairs. Lower is more stable. Reported as paired difference `Branch A \\u2212 Branch B`.\\n\\n**Co-secondary endpoints (S1, S2, S3)**\\n- **S1 \\u2014 Cross-seed loss-curve variance.** Standard deviation across the R=30 seeds of the integrated loss-trajectory AUC(\\u2207\\u00acL) at matched cell, paired.\\n- **S2 \\u2014 fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency.** \\u2016\\u2207L_fp32 \\u2212 cast(\\u2207L_fp64)\\u2016 / \\u2016\\u2207L_fp64\\u2016, evaluated on identical inputs and identical parameter tensors; serves as a consistency reference for S2 \\u2264 10\\u207b\\u2075 in fp64-imitating branches (sanity) and as a stability probe in fp32.\\n- **S3 \\u2014 Backward-only wall-clock and peak memory.** Reported as auxiliary per-phase attribution so that any aggregate time win can be attributed to the backward phase, the optimizer-step phase, or both.\\n\\n**Informational secondary endpoint (I-Perf \\u2014 per U6)**\\nWall-clock time under full 24-thread fan-out, unpinned, single-shot per cell. Reported only as an informational indicator; not part of the primary decision rule.\\n\\n### 1.3 Direction of effect\\n\\nP-Perf win: `(A)/(B) < 1` is desired. P-Stab win: `(A \\u2212 B) < 0` is desired. Both directed; pre-registered.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Comparator Definitions and API Contracts\\n\\n### 2.1 Branch A \\u2014 TorchOpt explicit-differentiation API\\n\\n**API-category interface contract (used as the source of truth in place of any fabricated symbol):**\\n\\nThe comparator is a \\\"functional / differentiable optimizer\\\" of the form:\\n\\n    meta_params = wrap(params)               # params re-bound as meta-params\\n    fast_params = tracer.trace_state(meta_params)\\n    for inner_step in range(L):\\n        loss_inner = functional_loss(fast_params, x_inner)\\n        fast_params = functional_step(fast_params, loss_inner)   # in-graph step\\n    loss_outer = outer_loss(model(fast_params), x_outer)\\n    loss_outer.backward()                    # gradients flow back through in-graph step\\n    outer_opt.step(meta_params)\\n\\nTwo properties are required by the contract and must be enforced by the eventual implementation:\\n\\n1. **In-graph reuse.** `functional_step` returns `fast_params` whose `.grad_fn` is a node in the autograd graph, and the saved tensors inside that node are *the same buffers used by the next iteration's forward*, not fresh clones.\\n2. **No re-materialization on the inner path.** The forward pass over the inner dataset is not re-evaluated when computing meta-gradients; the inverse-VJP is computed against the saved intermediates only. This is the operational meaning of \\\"reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation\\\" as it appears in the prompt.\\n\\n**Candidate symbol families (illustrative, NOT pre-registered as the exact API):** the `Meta*` family (e.g., the documented pattern of `MetaSGD` / `MetaAdam` / `MetaOptimizer`), the `RunMeta` family covering meta-gradient inner loops, and the lower-level `differentiable` functional-optimizer helpers. The exact symbol is **deferred to the real implementation** (see Open Items U1). What is fixed is the *interface contract above*, not the symbol.\\n\\n### 2.2 Branch B \\u2014 Standard PyTorch autograd + optim.step\\n\\n    y = model(x)\\n    loss = criterion(y, t)\\n    loss.backward()\\n    opt.step()\\n    opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=<toggleable>)\\n\\n`create_graph=False`. This is the pre-registered definition of \\\"standard autograd\\\" (see Open Items U2 for the meta-learning read of the question).\\n\\n### 2.3 Branch C \\u2014 Isolation control\\n\\nIdentical to Branch B except `loss.backward(create_graph=True)`. Purpose: separate the static cost of higher-order-graph construction from any explicit-diff reuse benefit. Branch C is the only one that builds a higher-order graph in either the standard side or the TorchOpt side; this arm disentangles \\\"allocations from graph build\\\" from \\\"allocations from inner-loop materialization\\\" so that the Branch-A win, if any, can be attributed correctly.\\n\\n### 2.4 API contracts that the implementation MUST honor\\n\\n- Identity-preserving optimizer state. A and B share the same base optimizer hyperparameters (lr, momentum, betas, eps, weight_decay) when one exists. Otherwise both are SGD with the same lr.\\n- Identity-preserving forward graph. No `torch.no_grad()`, no `.detach()` in either branch's hot loop, except as required by each branch's documented usage and explicitly enumerated in the confounds log.\\n- Identity-preserving init. Branch A and Branch B start from a shared state-dict that is held on the seed stream.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Independent Variables and Sweep Grid\\n\\n### 3.1 Variables\\n\\n| Variable | Levels | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Branch | A, B, C | Comparators |\\n| Model regime | MLP, small CNN, ill-conditioned quadratic | Three regimes: clean flow, convolution graph shape, deliberately bad conditioning |\\n| Batch size | {1, 16, 64, 256, 1024} | Per-sample \\u2192 large-batch scaling of allocator and reuse benefit |\\n| Inner-loop length L | {1, 5} | Tests \\\"single-step reuse\\\" vs. \\\"L-step unrolled reuse\\\"; L=5 is the meta-learning read of the question |\\n| Hessian condition \\u03ba | {10\\u00b2, 10\\u2074, 10\\u2076, 10\\u2078} | Applied only in the ill-conditioned-quadratic regime; tunes stability stress |\\n| Dtype | fp32 (timing), fp64 (consistency reference only) | Per U3 |\\n| Seeds | R = 30 | Per-seed paired power target: d \\u2265 0.5 at \\u03b1=0.05, power \\u2265 0.8 (Wilcoxon) |\\n| Perturbation directions M | 8 per seed | For P-Stab, separable RNG stream |\\n\\n### 3.2 Full factorial cell count\\n\\nA \\\"cell\\\" is one (branch, model, batch, L, dtype, [\\u03ba]) combination. The \\u03ba dimension applies only to the quadratic model.\\n\\n- MLP: 5 batches \\u00d7 2 L \\u00d7 1 dtype \\u00d7 30 seeds = 300 (timing) cells per branch\\n- CNN: 5 batches \\u00d7 2 L \\u00d7 1 dtype \\u00d7 30 seeds = 300 per branch\\n- Quadratic: 5 batches \\u00d7 2 L \\u00d7 1 dtype \\u00d7 4 \\u03ba \\u00d7 30 seeds = 1200 per branch\\n- Per branch: 1800 cells\\n- Three branches: 5400 cells\\n\\nEach cell runs once on the single-thread pinned protocol with W=5 warm-up iterations and K=50 timed iterations. The fp64 dtype is only used for the S2 consistency probe and is not included in timing cells. Timing-iteration count for the protocol:\\n\\n    total_iters = 5400 cells \\u00d7 30 seeds \\u00d7 (5 warm-up + 50 timed) iterations = 8,910,000 forward/backward/step calls\\n\\nIncluding L=5, Branch A and Branch C burn 5\\u00d7 the inner work per outer step.\\n\\n### 3.3 Minimally-feasible core (per U4)\\n\\nConsidering the CPU-only, 24-core, no-network, design-only constraint, the document defines a Core protocol that can be defended as a credible answer without running the full factorial:\\n\\n- One model regime \\u2014 the ill-conditioned quadratic (the most stability-sensitive regime and therefore the most diagnostic).\\n- Three \\u03ba values \\u2014 {10\\u00b2, 10\\u2076, 10\\u2078}. These cover \\\"easy\\\", \\\"challenging\\\", and \\\"very ill-conditioned\\\".\\n- Three batch sizes \\u2014 {1, 64, 1024}. These cover per-sample, mid, and large.\\n- Two L values \\u2014 {1, 5}. These separate the single-step reuse claim from the L-unrolled reuse claim.\\n- One branch set \\u2014 {A, B} only at Core level. Branch C is reported only at the Quadratic/\\u03ba=10\\u2076/(batch=64, L=1) cell to confirm the higher-order-graph isolation logic.\\n- Seeds R = 30.\\n- Dtype fp32.\\n\\nCore timing cells per branch: 3 \\u03ba \\u00d7 3 batches \\u00d7 2 L \\u00d7 30 seeds = 540. Pair (A vs B): 540 \\u00d7 2 = 1080 timed cells, plus the single 1-cell Branch C sanity check. Core iterations: 1080 \\u00d7 (5 + 50) = 59,400 calls. Including L=5 amplification on Branch A and Branch C: total Core work \\u2248 59,400 \\u00d7 (1 + \\u00bc\\u00b74) \\u2248 119k effective forward/backward calls. This is the size of the document's *committed* protocol; the full factorial remains the design's upper bound.\\n\\n**Pruning rationale.** The pruning keeps all models in the upper-bound specification but commits the Core to the regime most diagnostic of numerical stability (ill-conditioned quadratic), and keeps Batch \\u00d7 L coverage broad enough to demonstrate the L amplification effect. Models MLP and CNN are *retained* in the full factorial but moved to the \\\"informational\\\" tier so that the published analysis does not depend on them for the primary endpoint.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Stability Probes\\n\\n### 4.1 P-Stab: relative-weight perturbation sensitivity (PRIMARY, per U3)\\n\\nFor each seed s:\\n1. Train to a fixed checkpoint t* (pre-registered t* = 50 outer steps).\\n2. Clone \\u03b8 to \\u03b8\\u2032 and \\u03b8\\u2033 via separate RNG.\\n3. Direction e ~ UnitNormal(seed_s, m) with m = 1..M.\\n4. \\u03b8_p = \\u03b8 + \\u03b8 \\u2299 (\\u03b5 \\u00b7 e), where \\u03b5 = 1e-2 by default.\\n5. Compute sens(s, e) = \\u2016\\u2207L(\\u03b8_p) \\u2212 \\u2207L(\\u03b8)\\u2016 / \\u2016\\u2207L(\\u03b8)\\u2016.\\n6. Cell-level value = mean over M.\\n7. Branch-level value = mean over s.\\n\\nReported for both \\u03b5=1e-2 and \\u03b5=1e-4 (a coarse sweep). Lower is better.\\n\\n### 4.2 S1: cross-seed loss-curve variance\\n\\nEach cell computes R=30 trajectories of `(L_t, \\u2016\\u2207L_t\\u2016)` over T=200 outer steps. Reported endpoints: \\u03c3(AUC(L_t)) across seeds, paired; \\u03c3(AUC(\\u2016\\u2207L_t\\u2016)) across seeds, paired.\\n\\n### 4.3 S2: fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency\\n\\nRun a single forward/backward in fp32 and in fp64, on identical inputs. The fp64 gradient is the reference. Report `\\u2016\\u2207L_fp32 \\u2212 \\u2207L_fp64\\u2016 / \\u2016\\u2207L_fp64\\u2016`. Run once per cell. In Branch C, fp64 expected to be ~1e-7; fp32 expected to be ~1e-3 to 1e-7 depending on loss conditioning.\\n\\n### 4.4 Auxiliary stability probes\\n\\n- Gradient-norm trajectory smoothness: per-step \\u0394log\\u2016\\u2207L\\u2016 distribution.\\n- Hessian condition proxy (Hutchinson) at t*; reported as cell metadata, not an endpoint.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Performance and Memory Measurement Protocol\\n\\n### 5.1 Wall-clock (per U6 \\u2014 single-thread bound as PRIMARY)\\n\\n- Set `torch.set_num_threads(1)`, plus the equivalent NumPy/BLAS threading knobs via env vars (`OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1`).\\n- Pin to a single core via `taskset -c <core>` in the subprocess.\\n- Bracket two regions per iteration with `time.perf_counter_ns()`:\\n  - **Region R1.** `loss = criterion(model(x), t); loss.backward()` \\u2014 backward only.\\n  - **Region R2.** `loss.backward() ; opt.step() ; opt.zero_grad(...)` \\u2014 backward + step.\\n- W=5 un-timed warm-up iterations, then K=50 timed iterations.\\n- Per-seed summary: median and IQR of K values, separately for R1 and R2.\\n- Per-cell summary: median across R=30 seeds of the per-seed medians.\\n\\n**Informational 24-core wall-clock (I-Perf).** Same protocol except `torch.set_num_threads` returns to the system default and no pinning is applied. Reported as median ns/iter \\u00b1 IQR. Not part of the primary decision rule.\\n\\n### 5.2 Synchronization on CPU\\n\\nThere is no `cuda.synchronize` on CPU. The protocol uses `torch.set_num_threads(1)` + a tight barrier at the start of each timed iteration. Variance from the OS scheduler is absorbed by the K=50 repetitions and the median aggregate.\\n\\n### 5.3 Memory\\n\\nThree complementary measures per cell:\\n\\n1. **OS peak RSS.** `resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss` per subprocess. Subprocess isolation prevents allocator-cache carryover between cells, so RSS differences reflect the algorithm, not the cache.\\n2. **Python tracemalloc peak.** Start `tracemalloc.start()` after warm-up; snapshot at the end of the K=50 timed iterations; report peak and current.\\n3. **Retained-tensor bytes (estimate).** At the same checkpoint, snapshot `gc.get_objects()` filtered to `torch.Tensor` instances and sum `t.element_size() * t.nelement()` over each branch's live tensor set. Marked as estimate because `gc` walks are non-deterministic.\\n\\nA fourth, **allocator-namespace-specific** probe is included only where the implementation exposes a meaningful count (e.g., instrumented torchopt or a wrapped `cudaCachingAllocator` analogue for CPU). It is documented as a feature the protocol will use if available and as a no-op otherwise.\\n\\n### 5.4 Sequence isolation\\n\\nEach (cell, seed) pair is run in a fresh subprocess so that allocator state, autograd graph caches, and tracemalloc bookkeeping cannot leak across cells. Cell-level orchestration is the only place where numbers from different seeds are aggregated.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Statistical Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 6.1 Unit of analysis\\n\\nPer-seed paired. Each seed trains in parallel on A, B, and (when applicable) C, with identical init. Every aggregate statistic is computed on R=30 paired differences.\\n\\n### 6.2 Tests\\n\\n- **P-Perf.** Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-seed medians (paired). Bootstrap 95% CI on the median ratio `r_s = median_k(A)_s / median_k(B)_s`, B=10,000 resamples. Report paired Cohen's d_z and Cliff's \\u03b4 for effect size.\\n- **P-Stab.** Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-cell sens values. Bootstrap CI on the median paired difference. Effect sizes as above. Direction-preregistered: A \\u2212 B < 0 is a stability win.\\n- **S1, S2.** Wilcoxon signed-rank as for P-Stab; direction is \\\"smaller is better\\\".\\n- **S3 / I-Perf.** Reported but not hypothesis-tested in the primary analysis.\\n\\n### 6.3 Multiple-comparison correction\\n\\nThe published family is `(model \\u00d7 batch \\u00d7 \\u03ba \\u00d7 L \\u00d7 {P-Perf, P-Stab, S1, S2})`. Holm\\u2013Bonferroni correction is applied over this family. Family-wise \\u03b1 = 0.05.\\n\\n### 6.4 Pre-declared subgroup analyses\\n\\n- By batch size (interaction test: does the A-vs-B gap scale with batch?).\\n- By L (does the gap narrow or widen as the inner loop unrolls?).\\n- By \\u03ba in the quadratic regime (does the stability benefit increase with conditioning?).\\n\\n### 6.5 Sensitivity analyses\\n\\n- Drop the worst seed (highest Huber residual of medians across branches) and recompute the headlined effect.\\n- Drop the worst 10% of K=50 timed iterations within each seed and recompute.\\n- Reanalyze with `zero_grad(set_to_none=False)` data only and then with `set_to_none=True` only, to test the allocator-cache confound.\\n\\n### 6.6 Pre-registered decision rule (restated in \\u00a710)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Controls and Confounds\\n\\n| Confound | Mitigation |\\n|---|---|\\n| Different default dtype handling | Both branches run fp32 by default; fp64 reserved for S2 only. |\\n| Optimizer state mismatch | Same base optimizer in A and B; same hyperparameters. |\\n| Init mismatch | Shared state-dict per seed, cloned via `state_dict()` swap before training starts. |\\n| `zero_grad(set_to_none)` cache side-effect | Toggled; both settings reported; sensitivity analysis (\\u00a76.5). |\\n| Autograd graph cache state across cells | Subprocess isolation; warm-up 5 iterations per cell. |\\n| Forward-pass purity | No `no_grad`, no `detach` in the hot loop, except where the comparator contract requires it; enumerated. |\\n| Higher-order graph cost | Branch C isolation, required. |\\n| Scheduler jitter | Median over K=50 repetitions; single-thread pin in primary protocol. |\\n| MKL/BLAS thread dispersion | `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS` all set to 1 in primary. |\\n| Stability probe injection | Perturbation directions drawn from a perturbation-only RNG stream separated from the training RNG. |\\n| Hidden TorchOpt internal allocations | Documented in Open Items U1; the protocol can only report what is observable. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility and Runtime Budget\\n\\n**Environment.** CPU-only, 24 cores, no network. Pure-Python orchestration; no GPU; no dataset downloads.\\n\\n**Per-cell work in primary protocol (single thread).**\\n- One (cell, seed) pair runs K=55 iterations (5 warm-up + 50 timed) of forward + backward + step.\\n- With L=5 on Branches A and C, an additional 4 inner steps per outer step are inserted.\\n- Estimated per-iteration cost on the Core protocol at single-thread: ~5\\u201325 ms for the quadratic regime, ~10\\u201350 ms for MLP, ~20\\u201380 ms for CNN.\\n\\n**Core protocol cost (estimated at design stage; not measured).**\\n- 540 cells per branch \\u00d7 2 branches = 1080 cells \\u00d7 30 seeds = 32,400 (cell, seed) runs.\\n- Per run: average ~3 s wall-clock (W+K = 55 iterations at ~50 ms each).\\n- Total Core wall-clock at single-thread pin: 32,400 \\u00d7 3 s = ~27 hours single-thread. With 24 cores, perfect parallelism would bring this to ~1.1 hours; realistic utilization of ~70% puts it at ~1.6 hours. The Core protocol fits within a single CPU-day.\\n\\n**Full factorial cost.** 5400 cells \\u00d7 30 seeds \\u00d7 ~3 s \\u00f7 24 cores at 70% utilization \\u2248 ~8 hours CPU-time per-branch, ~24 hours total. Larger than Core but still \\u22641 CPU-day wall-clock at 24-core fan-out.\\n\\n**Cost of the fp64 consistency probe.** 5400 cells \\u00d7 1 fp64 call each. Negligible relative to the timing cells.\\n\\n**Cost of the stability probes.** P-Stab adds M=8 perturbation evaluations per cell, R1+R2-only; this is R1-only \\u00d7 M=8 \\u00d7 5400 cells = 43,200 single-region calls. Negligible relative to timing work.\\n\\n**Decision.** Core protocol is the published commitment. Full factorial is the upper bound retained for documentation. Branch C is restricted to a single-cell sanity confirmation in Core.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Threats to Validity\\n\\n1. **Device generalization.** The single-thread pinned protocol is the reproducibility anchor but does not reflect realistic 24-core or GPU behavior. Mitigation: I-Perf reports an informational 24-core number; per U6, that number is not the primary endpoint.\\n2. **TorchOpt version sensitivity.** No TorchOpt source is in scope, so the implementation of \\\"reuse intermediates\\\" may differ across TorchOpt point releases and the protocol may be measuring release-specific behavior. Mitigation: log the exact TorchOpt version, PyTorch version, and Python version in every run summary.\\n3. **Allocator library influence.** PyTorch's CPU allocator and OS malloc behavior affects tracemalloc and retained-tensor estimates. Mitigation: subprocess isolation and three complementary memory measures (\\u00a75.3), none of which an OS-level decision can invalidate independently.\\n4. **The \\\"reuse intermediate data\\\" benefit reinterpreted as allocator-cache warm-up.** A branch that reuses buffers may appear faster simply because it warms the malloc cache once and never re-touches free-list overhead. Mitigation: W=5 warm-up iterations plus the seed-replicated paired design; if the benefit survives warm-up, it cannot be solely attributable to first-call cache effects.\\n5. **The functional-optimizer contract may not perfectly match real symbol semantics.** No source quotation is possible (U1). Mitigation: the contract in \\u00a72.1 is the protocol's source of truth, not any specific symbol. A real execution must map the contract to the chosen symbol and disclose any deviation in the confounds log.\\n6. **Numerical stability under fp32 may be dominated by PyTorch's per-op kernels, not by the autograd graph.** Mitigation: include the ill-conditioned-quadratic regime at high \\u03ba, where the loss gradient amplifies any accumulated numerical error.\\n7. **Confounding between L=5 inner-loop unrolling and explicit-diff reuse benefit.** Mitigation: branch C in its single-cell sanity role, plus the L=1 vs L=5 subgroup analysis.\\n8. **OS-level background process interference.** Unmitigable but absorbed by the K-repetition median and the independent subprocess per cell.\\n9. **Single-CPU bias for BLAS.** The single-thread pin is required for reproducibility but underutilizes the 24-core budget for the timing kernel. Mitigation: I-Perf as the parallel arm.\\n10. **Implementation errors in the perturbation sensitivity probe.** Mitigation: print-assert that \\u2016\\u2207L(\\u03b8)\\u2016 is finite before dividing; clamp the per-cell value if it is not.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Pre-Registration of Primary Endpoint and Decision Rule\\n\\n### 10.1 Primary endpoint (singular, jointly pre-registered)\\n\\n- **P-Perf.** Per-iteration wall-clock of (backward + step) under single-thread pin, summarized as median-of-medians across R=30 seeds, reported as the ratio `r = median_A / median_B`. Direction-pre-registered: r < 1.\\n- **P-Stab.** Mean relative-weight perturbation sensitivity (\\u03b5=1e-2, M=8), per seed, summarized as median-of-means across R=30 seeds, reported as paired difference `d = mean_A \\u2212 mean_B`. Direction-pre-registered: d < 0.\\n\\n### 10.2 Decision rule\\n\\nFor each cell in the Core protocol, the protocol declares:\\n\\n- **TIME WIN** if and only if: `r_point < 1` AND `r_CI_high < 1` (two-sided 95% bootstrap CI on the median of `r_s` across seeds lies entirely below 1) AND Holm-corrected `p_Wilcoxon_Perf < 0.05`.\\n- **STABILITY WIN** if and only if: `d_point < 0` AND `d_CI_high < 0` (two-sided 95% bootstrap CI on the median of `d_s` lies entirely below 0) AND Holm-corrected `p_Wilcoxon_Stab < 0.05`.\\n- **BOTH WIN** claims the joint advantage.\\n- **INCONCLUSIVE** in all other cases \\u2014 including scenarios where direction matches but the CI crosses zero, or Holm correction nullifies an uncorrected finding.\\n\\n### 10.3 Pre-registration scope\\n\\nThe decision rule is locked at design time. Any deviation (e.g., adding a model regime, switching \\u03ba values, modifying \\u03b5) requires a new pre-registration record and is treated as a sensitivity analysis, not a primary test.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Open Items\\n\\n- **U1 \\u2014 TorchOpt API symbol resolution.** The comparator is described by interface contract, not by symbol. Candidate families (`Meta*`, `RunMeta`, `differentiable`) are illustrative only. A real implementation must select a symbol, document any contract deviation, and re-run the confounds log.\\n- **U2 \\u2014 Reading of the question.** Two readings are possible: (a) single backward+step with allocator-level reuse benefit, and (b) meta-learning inner-outer loop with in-graph differentiation through the optimizer. The protocol covers both by including L \\u2208 {1, 5} and the Branch C isolation arm. Confirmation of which reading the question intends is still open.\\n- **U3 \\u2014 Stability primary endpoint.** Per the researcher's instruction, the primary stability endpoint is perturbation sensitivity (S0 in this document); cross-seed variance (S1) and fp32-vs-fp64 gradient consistency (S2) are co-secondary. This choice is locked.\\n- **U4 \\u2014 Factorial size.** Per the researcher's instruction, the document preserves the full factorial as the upper bound and commits the published Core protocol to Quadratic \\u00d7 3 batches \\u00d7 3 \\u03ba \\u00d7 2 L. Pruning rationale is documented in \\u00a73.3 and \\u00a78. Any further pruning before publication is a pre-registration amendment.\\n- **U5 \\u2014 Memory primary metric.** The protocol reports three complementary memory numbers (RSS, tracemalloc peak, retained-tensor bytes). OS-peak RSS is the most reproducible across runs; tracemalloc peak is the most sensitive to allocator-level reuse; retained-tensor bytes is the most direct but is an estimate. The confusion matrix for these is documented but no single dominant metric is locked.\\n- **U6 \\u2014 Threading.** Per the researcher's instruction, single-thread pinned is the PRIMARY wall-clock protocol; 24-core wall-clock is INFORMATIONAL and secondary. This triage is pre-declared in \\u00a71.2, \\u00a75, \\u00a710.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Restatement of Design-Only Constraint\\n\\nThis document specifies an experiment. **No experiment was executed in producing this document.** All cell counts, timings, and effect sizes mentioned above are protocol parameters or design-stage projections, not measurements. The task context explicitly designates this as a design-only deliverable, and this document complies with that constraint throughout.\", \"evidence\": \"Inspection evidence from the planning phase remains controlling: /workspace contains only /workspace/task.json (521 B). No TorchOpt source, no README, no track.md is on disk. Python 3.12.3 is present but neither torch nor torchopt is importable, and the network is disabled, so even an exploratory import was impossible. The task.json quotes 'reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation' as the mechanism, declares the deliverable design-only ('Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'), and pins the runtime to CPU-only with 24 cores. Because no TorchOpt symbols exist on disk to quote, this design uses an interface contract (functional/differentiable optimizer taking meta-params and producing in-graph updated params) as the source of truth and lists Meta / RunMeta / differentiable families only as illustrative candidates, in line with U1. The Core protocol's pruning rationale (single model regime, three kappas, three batches, two L values, R=30 seeds, single branch set for primary test plus one Branch C sanity cell) is constructed to fit within the 24-core CPU no-network constraint and is documented in sections 3.3 and 8.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1: standard autograd means loss.backward(create_graph=False) plus optim.step(); create_graph=True defines Branch C as an isolation control, not the comparator.\", \"A2: TorchOpt explicit gradient differentiation API refers to the differentiable / functional-optimizer category; the exact symbol is described by an interface contract rather than a fabricated name and is deferred to a real implementation.\", \"A3: Allocator- and autograd-graph-level cost reductions measurable on CPU mirror those on GPU in a way that supports a CPU-only design as a credible comparator.\", \"A4: Numerical stability differences are most visible at float32 and at high Hessian condition number; float64 is the consistency reference for S2, not the primary stability probe.\"